01. Site-to-Site VPN

Connects an on-premises network to an AWS VPC over an encrypted VPN connection, typically using IPsec.

Real-time example:

Company On-Premises
10.10.0.0/16
       |
       | Encrypted VPN / Internet
       |
       v
AWS Site-to-Site VPN
       |
       v
Virtual Private Gateway
       |
       v
AWS VPC
10.0.0.0/16
       |
       +---- EC2
       +---- RDS
       +---- EKS

Example:

Your company has:

On-Prem
192.168.0.0/16

AWS:

VPC
10.0.0.0/16

You want an on-prem application to communicate with an AWS database:

On-Prem App
    |
    | VPN
    v
AWS VPC
    |
    v
RDS



02. AWS Direct Connect

Direct Connect provides a dedicated network connection from your on-premises network to AWS.

Conceptually:

Company Data Center
       |
       | Dedicated connection
       |
       v
AWS Direct Connect
       |
       v
AWS
       |
       v
VPC

Compared with VPN:

                 VPN                 Direct Connect
                 |                   |
On-Prem -------- Internet -------- AWS
   |                                  |
   |                                  |
   +---------- Dedicated ------------+

A simplified comparison:

	Site-to-Site VPN	Direct Connect
Connection	Internet-based	Dedicated network connection
Encryption	IPsec	Not inherently encrypted
Setup	Generally quicker	More involved
Latency	Internet-dependent	More predictable
Use case	Hybrid connectivity	Enterprise/high-throughput connectivity

Important: Direct Connect itself doesn't automatically encrypt traffic. If encryption is required, additional mechanisms such as MACsec (where supported) or VPN over Direct Connect may be used.




03. VPC Flow Logs

Very important for DevOps troubleshooting.

Example:

Application can't connect
       |
       v
Security Group?
       |
       v
NACL?
       |
       v
Route?
       |
       v
VPC Flow Logs
       |
       v
ACCEPT / REJECT

You'll use this when troubleshooting network connectivity.
